Every winter, our shop gets calls about pianos that suddenly sound out of tune or have sticky keys. The cause is almost always humidity. When indoor heating drops relative humidity below 35 percent, the wooden soundboard shrinks, string tension falls, and pitch drops noticeably in the middle of the keyboard.

{INJECTION}

The best protection is a stable environment. Aim for 40 to 50 percent relative humidity, keep the instrument away from radiators and exterior doors, and consider a climate control system installed inside the piano. Schedule tuning at least twice a year, ideally a few weeks after the heating season starts and again after it ends. If keys start sticking, avoid spraying lubricants and call a technician, since the problem is usually swollen or shrunken key bushings.

Our spring reissue shipment has arrived, and the jazz section is full. Highlights include a remastered pressing of a 1957 quartet date on 180-gram vinyl, cut from the original analog tapes, and a two-disc set of live club recordings from 1961 that has been out of print for decades.

{INJECTION}

We also have a limited run of a 1964 big band album with new liner notes and previously unseen session photographs. Prices range from 28 to 45 dollars, and members of our listening club get 10 percent off all reissues this month. The listening room is open on Saturday afternoons for anyone who wants to hear a record before buying. Stock is limited, so call ahead to reserve a copy of anything you have your eye on.

A good stand and bench matter more than most players expect. For home and gigging use, X-style stands are light and fold flat, but they can wobble under heavy playing. Z-style and table-style stands are more stable and leave room for your knees, at the cost of extra weight in the car.

{INJECTION}

For benches, adjustable height is the key feature. Your forearms should be roughly level with the keys, and a bench with a crank or knob adjustment lets you fine-tune your position between sessions or when sharing gear with other players. Padded tops are more comfortable over long sets. Expect to pay between 60 and 200 dollars for a solid stand and between 80 and 300 dollars for a bench that will last for years.

The Harborfront Jazz Festival returns for its 22nd year on the weekend of July 18. This year's program features 36 groups across three stages, including the main stage at the waterfront, a smaller club stage in the old customs house, and a free family stage in the park.

{INJECTION}

New this year is a late-night jam session hosted by the festival house band, open to registered musicians who sign up at the club stage by 9 p.m. Weekend passes cost 95 dollars, day tickets cost 50 dollars, and students receive a 30 percent discount with valid ID. The festival also runs two free workshops on Saturday morning, one on big band arranging and one on the business of booking gigs. Tickets go on sale next Tuesday.

This fall, the Westside Music School is adding three ear training classes for adult students. The beginner class covers intervals, major and minor triads, and simple melodic dictation. The intermediate class moves on to seventh chords, common progressions, and identifying chord qualities in recordings.

{INJECTION}

The advanced class focuses on harmonic analysis of jazz standards and soul tunes, with weekly listening assignments reviewed in small groups. Each class meets once a week in the evening for ten weeks, with a maximum of eight students. Tuition is 240 dollars per term, and current students receive priority registration until August 15. Placement is by a short online quiz, and the school offers a free trial lesson for anyone unsure which level fits.
